I’d choose Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, specifically a 4-bit MLX quantization, for that MacBook Air.

It is unusually well matched to what you described: technical conversation, explaining code/concepts, generating code, and reviewing existing code. Qwen3.6 has 35B total parameters but only ~3B active parameters per token, so you get much of the knowledge/capacity benefit of a 35B-class model without the inference cost of a dense 35B model. Qwen specifically calls out improved repository-level reasoning and coding in this release. 

Why I'd pick it
For coding, the published results are quite strong. Qwen reports 73.4 SWE-bench Verified, 49.5 SWE-bench Pro, 51.5 Terminal-Bench 2.0, and 80.4 LiveCodeBench v6. More importantly for your use, it isn't narrowly a code-completion model: its general knowledge and reasoning results remain strong, which matters when the job is “listen to a technical discussion, understand what's going on, and explain it to me” rather than simply writing functions. 

It also has a native 262K context window, potentially useful when feeding it substantial transcripts or source code. 

On your 32-GB M4 Air
I would run:

mlx-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-4bit

The current MLX 4-bit build is about 20.4 GB, so it fits comfortably enough in 32 GB unified memory while leaving roughly 11 GB for macOS, the KV cache, application memory, etc. 

I would not initially try to exploit the full 256K context window on a 32-GB machine. Start around 32K context, perhaps increase to 64K after seeing actual memory pressure. The model itself can handle vastly more; your RAM is the constraint. Qwen itself notes that context length should be reduced when memory is tight. 

There's an important detail here: Ollama's ordinary Q4_K_M distribution is about 24 GB, while the MLX 4-bit build is about 20.4 GB. On a 32-GB Air, I'd strongly prefer the latter because those ~3–4 GB of extra headroom are valuable. 

My ranking for your application
Model	My view
Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 4-bit MLX	Best choice
Gemma 4 31B Q4	Excellent general alternative, but less attractive for coding
Gemma 4 26B-A4B Q4	Very comfortable RAM footprint; good if responsiveness matters more
Qwen3.6 27B	Strong, but dense; I prefer the 35B-A3B architecture here
Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B	Good coding specialist, but superseded for your mixed use by Qwen3.6
Google estimates Gemma 4 31B Q4 at about 17.5 GB and the 26B-A4B Q4 at about 14.4 GB, so either would give you substantially more RAM headroom. 
 But Qwen3.6 is the one I'd sacrifice those extra few GB for, given that code generation/review is a central part of the workload.

One other reason I favor it for the listening-assistant system you've been designing: Qwen3.6 is multimodal and explicitly designed for long-context and agentic workflows. That gives us more room later to use the same model for transcript explanation, screenshots, coding help, tool calls, and repository inspection rather than assembling several unrelated specialist models. 

So my concrete recommendation is: M4 Air 32 GB → Qwen3.6-35B-A3B → MLX → 4-bit → initially ~32K context.

