Yes. For your machine and the assistant you’re building, I’d use this stack:

Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 4-bit MLX → LM Studio MLX runtime → llmster headless server → OpenAI-compatible API.

LM Studio’s MLX engine is built on Apple’s MLX ecosystem (mlx-lm/mlx-vlm), so you retain the Apple-Silicon-native execution path rather than falling back to GGUF/llama.cpp. 

Why LM Studio rather than raw mlx_lm.server
If I were merely benchmarking models interactively, I’d be happy with raw mlx-lm. For the actual application, though, LM Studio gives you a much better service boundary without giving up MLX.

You get a proper headless daemon called llmster, explicitly intended to run LM Studio without its GUI. It can run as a background service and load models on demand. 

You also get all three interfaces:

native LM Studio REST API

OpenAI-compatible API

Anthropic-compatible API

The OpenAI-compatible interface includes /v1/chat/completions, /v1/responses, embeddings, structured output and tool use. 

That means I'd architect your application against an OpenAI-style interface rather than against MLX directly:

audio / transcript / screenshot / code
                │
                ▼
       your assistant process
                │
        OpenAI-compatible HTTP
                │
                ▼
        LM Studio / llmster
                │
            MLX engine
                │
                ▼
      Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 4-bit
That abstraction is valuable because you can later substitute another local model—or even a remote model—without rewriting the application.

Another big advantage for your two-machine idea
LM Studio can serve either on localhost or over the local network. Its CLI also supports operating on an LM Studio instance on another machine. 

So your architecture naturally becomes:

ONE-MAC MODE

MacBook Air
 ├── meeting assistant
 ├── transcription
 └── LM Studio + Qwen
or:

TWO-MAC MODE

Meeting Mac                      Companion Mac
───────────                      ─────────────
meeting assistant ─── LAN ───► LM Studio
transcription                    Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
screenshots                      32 GB unified RAM
Your application doesn't fundamentally care which arrangement it's running under. You just change:

http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
to something like:

http://companion-mac.local:1234/v1
That is particularly attractive for the single-Mac/two-Mac architecture you wanted.

Installation I would use
You don't actually need the LM Studio GUI for the finished system. LM Studio recommends llmster for headless operation on Mac/Linux: 

curl -fsSL https://lmstudio.ai/install.sh | bash

lms daemon up
Then search specifically for MLX versions:

lms get qwen3.6-35b-a3b --mlx
lms get --mlx restricts results to MLX models. 

After downloading:

lms ls
Find the exact model key and initially load it with:

lms load <model-key> \
    --context-length 32768 \
    --identifier meeting-assistant
LM Studio lets you explicitly set context length and also has a memory estimator, so before trying 64K you can do:

lms load --estimate-only <model-key> \
    --context-length 65536
The estimator accounts for context length and other model characteristics. 

Then:

lms server start
Your program can then be almost boring
For example, Python can use the ordinary OpenAI client while talking entirely to your Mac: 

from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI(
    base_url="http://localhost:1234/v1",
    api_key="local"
)

response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="meeting-assistant",
    messages=[
        {
            "role": "system",
            "content": """
You are a technical meeting assistant.
Explain unfamiliar technical concepts concisely.
For code, identify bugs and implementation implications.
"""
        },
        {
            "role": "user",
            "content": transcript_fragment
        }
    ],
)

print(response.choices[0].message.content)
That same code can talk to the second Mac simply by changing base_url.

One LM Studio feature is especially useful on 32 GB
It supports idle TTL and automatic model eviction. You can, for example, load the 35B model and tell LM Studio to release its ~large unified-memory allocation after an hour of inactivity:

lms load <model-key> \
    --context-length 32768 \
    --ttl 3600
The idle timer resets whenever the model receives another request. 

That's much nicer on a 32-GB daily-use laptop than permanently losing most of your unified memory merely because the server happens to be running.

Why not Ollama?
Ollama is excellent, and it now directly offers qwen3.6:35b-a3b; its current package is about 24 GB and supports the model's 256K context capability. Ollama also accelerates Apple Silicon through Metal. 

I wouldn't choose it here because your situation strongly favors MLX-native models, and LM Studio gives you MLX plus the operational conveniences I want for your application.

I would rank the choices:

Stack	For you
LM Studio + MLX + llmster	★★★★★
raw mlx-lm	★★★★☆
Ollama	★★★★☆
llama.cpp directly	★★★☆☆
Raw mlx-lm is the one I would keep around as a benchmark/reference implementation. Apple's MLX-LM package is deliberately focused on running and fine-tuning LLMs on Apple Silicon, and its maintainers describe mlx_lm.server primarily as a local HTTP endpoint rather than a larger production serving platform. 

One change from my previous recommendation
I'd actually start with 32K context and test 16K as well, rather than assuming that more context is always better.

For the real-time meeting assistant, you don't want to continually resend an enormous meeting transcript. The better architecture is:

raw transcript archive
        │
        ├── recent verbatim window ──┐
        │                            │
        └── rolling summary ─────────┼──► LLM
                                     │
code/screenshots/relevant history ──┘
That should dramatically reduce time-to-first-token, memory consumed by KV cache, and repeated prompt processing. The Qwen model itself supports a huge context, but your 32-GB Air shouldn't be asked to exercise it routinely. There are current reports of memory pressure/OOM with Qwen3.6-35B-A3B MLX at long context on Apple Silicon, reinforcing the case for intentionally controlling context. 

So my production choice would be: llmster headless + LM Studio MLX runtime + Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 4-bit + 16–32K working context + OpenAI-compatible API.

And I think the next architectural choice is even more consequential for responsiveness: how to divide work between a tiny fast model and this 35B model. For example, transcript triage/routing can potentially happen with a 3–8B model while only difficult explanations and code analysis go to Qwen3.6-35B. That could make the meeting assistant feel much closer to instantaneous on an M4 Air.


