Yes—but I would use the specialized model for hearing/transcribing the conversation, not for interpreting its meaning.

I’d split the system into layers:

recorded/live audio
      │
      ▼
specialized speech model
  transcription + timestamps
      │
      ├── speaker diarization
      ▼
speaker-labelled transcript
      │
      ▼
Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
  ├── explain conversation
  ├── identify technical concepts
  ├── explain jokes/idioms/ambiguities
  ├── summarize decisions
  ├── generate code
  └── review code
For your particular application, I'd start with Whisper large-v3-turbo as the speech model, running through MLX on the Mac. Whisper Turbo is an 809M-parameter model derived from large-v3; OpenAI describes it as substantially faster than large-v3 with only a small accuracy loss, and it remains multilingual. 
 Apple maintains a Whisper implementation in its MLX examples specifically for Apple Silicon. 

There is an especially important reason I'd initially choose Whisper over an otherwise very attractive alternative such as NVIDIA Parakeet: technical vocabulary. Whisper accepts an initial_prompt specifically so you can supply context, custom vocabulary and proper nouns to bias transcription toward the correct terminology. 
 Thus, before a meeting you could feed the ASR layer something roughly like:

Kubernetes, DuckDB, Parquet, WebLogic, Qwen,
Interactive Brokers, pyannote, MLX, LangSmith...
That capability could be quite valuable when a slightly garbled technical term would subsequently cause the LLM to misunderstand the entire exchange.

I would add a second specialist: diarization
For recorded conversations, knowing who said what can materially improve interpretation. I'd use pyannote speaker-diarization-community-1. It is specifically designed to turn audio into speaker-labelled segments, can run completely offline, and its new "exclusive speaker diarization" output is intended to make reconciliation with speech-recognition timestamps easier. Pyannote reports it as substantially improved over its previous 3.1 model on most of its diarization benchmarks. 

So instead of giving Qwen this:

I think we should change the cache.
No, that's probably too expensive.
What about doing it per ticker?
That might work.
you give it:

[00:14:21] SPEAKER_1:
I think we should change the cache.

[00:14:25] SPEAKER_2:
No, that's probably too expensive.

[00:14:29] SPEAKER_1:
What about doing it per ticker?

[00:14:32] SPEAKER_2:
That might work.
That is much better source material for questions such as "What did Alice disagree with?", "What solution did they settle on?", or "Explain Bob's objection."

There is one very interesting alternative: Parakeet
I'd definitely benchmark NVIDIA Parakeet-TDT-0.6B-v3 against Whisper before freezing the architecture.

It is only 600M parameters and is explicitly designed for high-throughput ASR. It gives punctuation, capitalization, and word-level and segment-level timestamps, supports long recordings, and handles 25 European languages including English, French, and German. 

More interestingly, NVIDIA now provides NeMo-Speech.cpp, a lightweight C++ runtime with a Metal backend. It can run locally as a server and even provides an OpenAI-compatible audio API and real-time WebSocket transcription. 

So I would test:

                     ┌─ Whisper large-v3-turbo / MLX
Audio ── ASR interface ┤
                     └─ Parakeet 0.6B-v3 / NeMo-Speech.cpp
and keep that interface abstract. Then changing the transcription engine doesn't affect the rest of your application.

Whisper gets my initial vote because of technical-vocabulary prompting. Parakeet gets my vote as the first challenger because of its efficiency and excellent timestamp-oriented transcription architecture.

What about an LLM that listens directly to the audio?
This is where I'd not make it the primary pipeline—at least initially.

There are now models such as Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B that accept audio directly and reason about it. Qwen says it is designed for audio understanding as well as text/image/video, including real-time audio interaction. There is even a specialized Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Captioner intended for fine-grained analysis of complex audio. 

That can potentially recover information a transcript discards:

hesitation
sarcasm
laughter
interruptions
raised voice
uncertainty
long pauses
background sounds
But I would use it as an optional second-pass audio analyzer, not as the routine transcription/reasoning engine. On a 32-GB MacBook Air, running another 30B-class multimodal model alongside your 35B reasoning model is an unattractive use of memory; Qwen itself notes that disabling just the speech-output component of Qwen3-Omni saves about 10 GB of GPU memory. 

So the eventual architecture I'd aim for is:

                     ┌─────────────────────────────┐
Audio ──────────────►│ Whisper/Parakeet ASR        │
                     └────────────┬────────────────┘
                                  │ transcript
                     ┌────────────▼────────────────┐
Audio ──────────────►│ pyannote diarization        │
                     └────────────┬────────────────┘
                                  │
                                  ▼
                       enriched conversation
                                  │
                                  ▼
                        Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
                     reasoning / explanation /
                       coding / code review
                                  │
                 difficult audio? │
                                  ▼
                       Qwen3-Omni optional
                        audio second opinion
This also has a very nice property for your one-Mac/two-Mac design: Whisper/Parakeet and diarization are relatively small, so the meeting Mac can do audio processing locally while the 35B model lives either on that same Mac or on the companion machine. 

One caveat I'd explicitly test is Swiss German. Parakeet lists German but not Swiss German among its 25 supported languages, and Whisper's standard language set similarly treats German as the language rather than exposing Swiss German as a separate target. 
 Given how important understanding Swiss German could be in your use case, I'd create a small real-world Swiss-German test corpus and compare Whisper, Parakeet, and potentially a Swiss-German-specialized ASR model before making the final ASR choice.

So my current recommendation becomes three models with very different jobs, rather than three competing LLMs:

Whisper Turbo → pyannote → Qwen3.6-35B-A3B.

And I would keep Qwen3-Omni as an optional later enhancement for cases where understanding how something was said matters, rather than just what was said.


